\section*{Capturing polynomial time with choiceless computation and counting}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 243; 2026-09-23), the asserted result is that a fixed linear-consistency query over $\mathbb F_3$ is polynomial-time decidable but not definable in choiceless polynomial time with counting on all unordered finite structures.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
The finite vocabulary has eight binary relations $\mathsf{Ed},\mathsf{Cf},\mathsf{EB},\mathsf{VB},\mathsf I,\mathsf Z_0,\mathsf Z_1,\mathsf Z_2$. In a structure $A$, set $Y=\{y:\mathsf{Ed}(y,y)\}$, $X=\{a:\mathsf{Cf}(a,a)\}$ and $X_t=\{a\in X:\mathsf{VB}(t,a)\}$. Over $\mathbb F_3$ put
\[
 C(y,a)=\sum_{\substack{x\in Y\\\mathsf I(a,x)}}\ \sum_{\substack{\delta\in\mathbb F_3\\\mathsf Z_{\delta}(y,x)}}\delta.
\]
Define $Q(A)$ as consistency of unknowns $(\lambda_a)_{a\in X},(\mu_y)_{y\in Y}$ satisfying
\[
 \sum_{a\in X_t}\lambda_a=1\ (t\in X),\qquad
 \mu_y=\sum_{a\in X_t}\lambda_a C(y,a)
\]
for those pairs $(t,y)$ having some $a\in X_t,x\in Y$ with $\mathsf I(a,x)$ and $\mathsf{EB}(y,x)$. The claim is that $Q$ is an isomorphism-invariant polynomial-time query on all finite structures while no sentence of CPT with counting defines $Q$, even when set ranks are unbounded.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 243; 2026-09-23), charged three-dimensional grids have opposite consistency answers. Central-extension automorphisms force small constructed objects to have sparse supports, and counting-equivalent grids preserve every fixed-program state and output despite their different total charges.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The displayed question has a negative answer under the manuscript's stated construction. Its counterexample meets the conditions in this problem and defeats the universal assertion, so this is a full disproof claim. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 243; 2026-09-23).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 243; 2026-09-23), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: a fixed linear-consistency query over $\mathbb F_3$ is polynomial-time decidable but not definable in choiceless polynomial time with counting on all unordered finite structures.

The central assertion is within the documented formal scope; the additional applications retain their own scope limits.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{ChoicelessPolynomialTime.lean}, \texttt{WitnessedChoice.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.CPTSeparation.main}, \texttt{OAI.WitnessedChoice.main}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

